<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/336-bce">336</a>–<a href="/years/323-bce">323 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Alexander III of Macedon · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argead-dynasty">Argead dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander the Great, Alexander III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, was king of Macedon from 336 to 323 BCE. He conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and led his army as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley, creating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>. After his death his generals divided his conquests, founding successor states such as the Ptolemaic and Seleucid empires, and his campaigns spread Greek culture across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="Alexander the Great" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The decisive military collapse came only with <b>Alexander the Great</b>'s campaigns (334–330 BCE), when a Macedonian army of exceptional tactical sophistication defeated Persian forces at the Granicus, Issus and Gaugamela in rapid succession.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Nader Shah's military achievements rank among the most remarkable in the history of warfare, and historians have often compared him with <b>Alexander the Great</b>, Genghis Khan and Timur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Antigonus I Monophthalmus, ‘the One-Eyed’, one of <b>Alexander the Great</b>'s senior officers, had governed Phrygia since 333 BCE, and in the wars of the Successors after 323 BCE he built up the largest of the competing realms, stretching from western Asia Minor to Syria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The tactics of Leuctra, with a deep, weighted wing and the coordination of infantry and cavalry, were studied by later commanders from Philip and <b>Alexander the Great</b> to modern military theorists.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its ships nonetheless had military value: in 332 BCE its king, Enylus, joined <b>Alexander the Great</b> with his squadron during the siege of Tyre after learning that Byblos had already submitted to the Macedonians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A letter of <b>Alexander the Great</b>, preserved in an inscription and probably written between 334 and 332 BCE, orders the return of exiles and the drafting of a democratic constitution.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pella, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.76,22.53&amp;year=-330" aria-label="Pella on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5102 708 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alexander the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5552 978h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5552 978h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.76,22.53&amp;year=-330">What was here in 330 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Glorious are the deeds of those who undergo labour and run the risk of danger; and it is delightful to live a life of valour and to die leaving behind immortal glory.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at the Hyphasis (Arrian, Anabasis, book 5) &#183; tr. E. J. Chinnock, 1884 &#183; Arrian's account of his speech to his officers &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46976/pg46976-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Come now! whoever of you has wounds, let him strip and show them, and I will show mine in turn; for there is no part of my body, in front at any rate, remaining free from wounds…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at Opis (Arrian, Anabasis, book 7) &#183; tr. E. J. Chinnock, 1884 &#183; Arrian's account of his speech to his army &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46976/pg46976-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I, for my part, think, that to a brave man there is no end to labours except the labours themselves…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at the Hyphasis (Arrian, Anabasis, book 5) &#183; tr. E. J. Chinnock, 1884 &#183; Arrian's account of his speech to his officers &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46976/pg46976-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For my part, I assure you, I had rather excel others in the knowledge of what is excellent, than in the extent of my power and dominion.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Aristotle (Plutarch, Life of Alexander) &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; complaining that his teacher had published his lectures &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;This, it seems, is royalty.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Alexander &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; on entering the luxurious tent of the Persian king Darius III, captured after Issus &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
